!! What is SSRF (in very simple words)?
SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) means:

👉 Instead of you sending a request
👉 You make the server send the request for you

If the application is not careful, we can tell the server:
“Hey, go talk to this internal page.”

And the server actually does it 😅

? What was happening in this lab?
The website was a shopping site.
When I clicked on a product, there was a stock check feature at the bottom.

Behind the scenes:

The app was calling another URL to check stock
That URL was passed as a parameter
The server trusted that value fully ❌
That’s where the problem started.

@@ Goal of the Lab
The goal was:

👉 Use SSRF to access the internal admin page
👉 That admin page was running on localhost

Normal users should never be able to access it.

? How I Solved It (My Understanding)
1️⃣ Watching the request
When I clicked “Check stock”, I intercepted the request using Burp Suite.

I noticed a parameter that contained a URL (stock API).

This was a big hint.

2️⃣ Decoding the URL
The URL was encoded, so I decoded it to understand what the server was calling.

Become a member
After decoding, I saw that the server was fetching stock data from another internal service.

3️⃣ Changing the URL
Instead of letting the server go to the stock service, I changed the URL to point to:

http://localhost/admin
Since the server trusted my input, it made a request to its own admin page.

And it worked 😮

4️⃣ Accessing the Admin Page
Once the admin page was visible, I could see admin actions that should never be exposed to users.

After performing the required admin action, the lab was marked as solved 🎉

? What I Learned from This Lab
SSRF happens when user input controls server requests
Internal services are often trusted too much
URL encoding does not make input safe
Admin pages must have strong access controls
SSRF can be very dangerous if ignored
?Why SSRF is Dangerous in Real Life
If SSRF exists in a real application, attackers can:

Access internal admin panels
Scan internal networks
Reach cloud metadata services
Steal sensitive information
That’s why SSRF is a high-impact vulnerability.

